What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_voltageY_input_mux
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		What channel Y is connected to, the MUX field of its CHnSET
		register. One of the values in in_voltage_input_mux_available:

		  * "normal"           - the channel's electrode inputs.
		  * "shorted"          - both inputs tied together, for an
		    offset or noise measurement.
		  * "bias_measurement" - the BIASIN pin; the driver sets
		    BIAS_MEAS while any channel is here.
		  * "supply"           - a supply of the part (see
		    in_voltage_mvdd_analog_raw).
		  * "temperature"      - the internal temperature sensor.
		  * "test_signal"      - the internal test generator. Refused
		    with -EINVAL while test_signal is "off": the part has no
		    external test pin, so the input would read a node that
		    nothing drives.
		  * "bias_drive_p", "bias_drive_n" - the bias drive routed out
		    through the positive or the negative electrode.

		A write is refused with -EBUSY while the buffer is enabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_voltage_input_mux_available
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		The values in_voltageY_input_mux accepts, the same for every
		channel.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_voltageY_srb2
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		1 closes the switch between the SRB2 pin and channel Y's
		positive input, 0 opens it. A write is refused with -EBUSY
		while the buffer is enabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/montage
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/montage_available
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		The SRB1 switches, which tie the SRB1 pin to the negative input
		of every channel whose input_mux is "normal", "bias_drive_p" or
		"bias_drive_n":

		  * "bipolar"     - open: each channel measures between its own
		    two electrodes.
		  * "referential" - closed: each channel measures its positive
		    electrode against what SRB1 carries, usually one common
		    reference electrode.

		Only the switches change; the bias derivation and the lead-off
		sensing are left as they are. A write is refused with -EBUSY
		while the buffer is enabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/test_signal
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/test_signal_available
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		The internal test generator, which reaches a channel whose
		input_mux is "test_signal":

		  * "off"                          - the generator is off.
		  * "1x_slow", "1x_fast", "1x_dc"  - an amplitude of
		    (VREFP - VREFN) / 2400, as a square wave at fCLK / 2^21 or
		    fCLK / 2^20, or held at DC.
		  * "2x_slow", "2x_fast", "2x_dc"  - twice that amplitude.

		"off" is refused with -EINVAL while any channel is on the test
		input. A write is refused with -EBUSY while the buffer is
		enabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/bias_drive
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/bias_drive_available
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		"on" powers the bias drive amplifier, "off" powers it down.
		What it drives follows from in_voltageY_bias_sense_p and
		in_voltageY_bias_sense_n: the inverted average of the inputs
		selected, or with none selected just BIASREF, which is
		(AVDD + AVSS) / 2 unless the board feeds the BIASREF pin. A
		write is refused with -EBUSY while the buffer is enabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_voltageY_bias_sense_p
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_voltageY_bias_sense_n
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		1 adds channel Y's positive (sense_p) or negative (sense_n)
		input to the inputs averaged into the bias drive, 0 takes it
		out. Selecting inputs does not power the amplifier, nor does
		powering it select any. A write is refused with -EBUSY while
		the buffer is enabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_voltageY_lead_off_sense_p
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_voltageY_lead_off_sense_n
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		1 turns lead-off detection on for channel Y's positive
		(sense_p) or negative (sense_n) input, 0 turns it off. The
		lead-off comparators are powered while any input, or the bias
		electrode, is sensed. A write is refused with -EBUSY while the
		buffer is enabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_voltageY_lead_off_flip
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		0 pulls channel Y's positive input to AVDD and its negative
		input to AVSS for lead-off detection, 1 swaps the two. A write
		is refused with -EBUSY while the buffer is enabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/lead_off_threshold_percent
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/lead_off_threshold_percent_available
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		The lead-off comparator thresholds, as the positive side's
		threshold in percent of the supply; the negative side's is its
		complement, so "95" is 95% and 5%. One of "95", "92.5", "90",
		"87.5", "85", "80", "75", "70". It applies to every lead-off
		detection on the part. A write is refused with -EBUSY while the
		buffer is enabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/lead_off_current_nanoamp
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/lead_off_current_nanoamp_available
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		The lead-off excitation current in nanoamperes: "6", "24",
		"6000" or "24000". It applies to every lead-off detection on
		the part. A write is refused with -EBUSY while the buffer is
		enabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/lead_off_frequency
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/lead_off_frequency_available
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		The lead-off excitation:

		  * "dc"           - DC; the comparators report the state.
		  * "ac_7.8", "ac_31.2" - AC at about 7.8 Hz or 31.2 Hz, from
		    the 2.048 MHz internal clock (fCLK / 2^18 and fCLK / 2^16;
		    an external clock scales them).
		  * "ac_fdr_div_4" - AC at a quarter of the data rate.

		With AC excitation the electrode is seen in the data, as the
		amplitude at the excitation frequency; the comparators do not
		report it, so lead_off_status reads "ac", the lead-off scan
		element reads 0 and no lead-off event is raised. A write is
		refused with -EBUSY while the buffer is enabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/lead_off_status
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		The lead-off comparators' state, read from the part:

		  * "disabled" - no input is sensed.
		  * "ac"       - the excitation is AC (see lead_off_frequency).
		  * two hexadecimal bytes, positive inputs then negative, as
		    "PP NN" - bit n set when channel n + 1's input is off. Only
		    sensed inputs are reported; the others read 0.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/scan_elements/in_voltageX_lead_off_en
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/scan_elements/in_voltageX_lead_off_index
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/scan_elements/in_voltageX_lead_off_type
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		A scan element after the voltage channels that carries each
		conversion's lead-off state: an unsigned 16-bit value,
		(positive << 8) | negative, bit n of each byte set when
		channel n + 1's input is off. Only sensed inputs are reported,
		and the value is 0 with AC excitation. It has no _raw and no
		_scale: it is not a voltage.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/events/fault_openwire_en
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		1 raises an IIO_EV_TYPE_FAULT / IIO_EV_DIR_FAULT_OPENWIRE event
		on in_voltageY when one of channel Y's sensed inputs comes off,
		once per transition, with the timestamp of the conversion that
		showed it. Events come from the status word of each conversion,
		so only while the buffer is enabled, and never with AC
		excitation. 0, the default, raises none.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/bias_lead_off
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		"on" turns lead-off detection on for the bias electrode, and
		the lead-off comparators with it; "off" turns it off. With the
		bias drive off the lead-off current goes into the electrode;
		with it on, the comparator watches the amplifier's output
		against the lead_off_threshold_percent thresholds. A write is
		refused with -EBUSY while the buffer is enabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/bias_lead_off_status
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		The bias electrode's lead-off state, read from the part:
		"disabled" while bias_lead_off is "off", otherwise "connected"
		or "disconnected".

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_voltage_mvdd_analog_raw
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_voltage_mvdd_analog_scale
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_voltage_mvdd_digital_raw
What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/in_voltage_mvdd_digital_scale
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		The voltage a channel reads with its input_mux at "supply",
		taken on channel 1 (analog) and channel 3 (digital) at gain 1
		and with the channel put back as it was. raw * scale is in
		millivolts. The datasheet gives it as 0.5 x (AVDD + AVSS) on
		channels 1, 2 and 5 to 8 and as DVDD / 4 on channels 3 and 4;
		the voltage measured is reported and no supply is inferred
		from it. A read is refused with -EBUSY while the buffer is
		enabled.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/lost_samples
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Conversions the host did not collect since the buffer was last
		enabled: each one that became due while the read of an earlier
		one was still in flight, past the one that read then catches.

What:		/sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceX/timestamp_source
KernelVersion:	6.6
Contact:	linux-iio@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		Where the timestamp of each buffered conversion comes from:

		  * "drdy_irq"   - the DRDY interrupt; the timestamp is taken
		    one SPI transfer after the conversion.
		  * "host_timer" - a host timer, when DRDY cannot reach the host
		    as an interrupt; the timestamp is the host's request, one
		    bus round trip after the conversion.
